      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by their quoted evidence. Tier assignments are appropriate \u2014 measurable claims draw on Electoral Commission data and academic research rather than projections. The E6 claim (1.3% / half a million) involves some modelling, which the rationale acknowledges. Disproportionate impact claim (E8) is slightly incomplete as the quote doesn't mention young people or ethnic minorities, but E9 covers those groups and the rationale cites both; the claim as written only references the groups named in E8 so this is acceptable. No lever leakage \u2014 the verdict correctly judges voter ID's coercive effect on civic liberty rather than its electoral integrity merits. No party-blind issues \u2014 the rationale avoids motive attribution and assesses the structural effect. The Electoral Commission source is listed as source_type 'media' when it is an institutional/government body \u2014 minor metadata error but not a blocking issue. Direction of 'improves' is supported by claims on both measures. No false balance concern \u2014 the verdict does not reach mixed/too-uncertain. Magnitude of moderate is defensible given sub-1% electorate affected per election. No blocking issues found."
